Official RAFT commit: bfe2320f5d719d1e2a024330de3410b370bbb2a9
Reason: the official model moves `index` to CUDA and then uses it to index the
CPU-resident `retrieval_dict`, which fails under PyTorch 2.5.1. Retrieval output
is moved to the model device immediately afterward by the unchanged code.

--- a/models/RAFT.py
+++ b/models/RAFT.py
@@
-        index = index.to(self.device)
+        # Compatibility fix: retrieval_dict is intentionally stored on CPU.
+        # PyTorch requires the advanced index to be on the same device.
+        index = index.cpu()

Second compatibility/correctness fix for 70/10/20 custom splits: the official
training loop evaluates both validation and test data through `mode='valid'`.
This is hidden when validation and test have equal sizes, but otherwise indexes
the validation retrieval tensor with test indices. The patch threads an explicit
retrieval mode into `vali` and uses `test` for the test loader.

--- a/exp/exp_long_term_forecasting.py
+++ b/exp/exp_long_term_forecasting.py
@@
-    def vali(self, vali_data, vali_loader, criterion):
+    def vali(self, vali_data, vali_loader, criterion, retrieval_mode='valid'):
@@
-                    outputs = self.model(batch_x, index, mode='valid')
+                    outputs = self.model(batch_x, index, mode=retrieval_mode)
@@
-            vali_loss = self.vali(vali_data, vali_loader, criterion)
-            test_loss = self.vali(test_data, test_loader, criterion)
+            vali_loss = self.vali(vali_data, vali_loader, criterion, retrieval_mode='valid')
+            test_loss = self.vali(test_data, test_loader, criterion, retrieval_mode='test')
